WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.392
You now have the NumPy foundation needed for the next stage of the course.

00:00:06.392 --> 00:00:11.687
If you want additional practice, the official NumPy learning resources and user guide are excellent

00:00:11.687 --> 00:00:14.757
references.

00:00:14.757 --> 00:00:20.212
The beginner materials are especially useful because they reinforce arrays, indexing, shapes, broadcasting,

00:00:20.212 --> 00:00:23.542
and numerical operations using short examples.

00:00:23.542 --> 00:00:31.977
The slide also includes examples of data-science roles where NumPy appears alongside tools such as Pandas,

00:00:31.977 --> 00:00:34.687
scikit-learn, SQL, Jupyter, and visualization libraries.

00:00:34.687 --> 00:00:42.862
For your own practice, take a numerical problem you previously solved with Python lists and loops and rewrite

00:00:42.862 --> 00:00:45.217
it with a NumPy array.

00:00:45.217 --> 00:00:52.072
For example, calculate averages, filter values above a threshold, standardize measurements,

00:00:52.072 --> 00:00:54.642
or summarize several months of sales.

00:00:54.642 --> 00:00:57.137
Then compare the two approaches.

00:00:57.137 --> 00:01:01.327
In Module 10, we will build on these ideas with Pandas,

00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:08.657
where rows and columns receive labels and we gain tools for real table-shaped datasets, cleaning, grouping,

00:01:08.657 --> 00:01:09.704
and analysis.
